## #2 / #10 / #11 — one reader (do after the v1.27 baseline)

### Root cause (verified)

`OfficeView` renders through its own private `OfficeText`
(`src/ui/OfficeView.tsx:35`): bare `<span>` per line, no `data-line`, no echo
class, no handlers, hand-rolled `<div className="bilingual">`, and a duplicate
`bangLineClass` that already exists at `src/ui/BilingualText.tsx:24`. The
machinery was never mounted in Breviary.

The rendering primitives are correct and already shared. In `TextLines`
(`BilingualText.tsx:118`) a corpus line beginning with `!` takes an early
return — rendered as `<span className="rubric-text">` with **no `data-line`, no
echo class, and no trailing newline** — while ordinary lines get `data-line={i}`,
`xlate-echo` when hovered, and *do* emit `'\n'`. That asymmetry is why the
rubric flows inline as a red italic prefix yet cannot receive the highlight:
**only the spoken sentence lights up**, which is the whole point of following
your place. Sentence granularity comes from the corpus itself — DO already puts
each spoken sentence on its own line. No segmentation needs building.

The **interaction** layer is the problem: align + callout + `Menu` +
`SelectionAction` live inside `ReaderView` (`:31`, `:50`, `:75`, `:266`, `:398`,
`:442`, `:572`); `BibleView` carries a second copy (`:60`, `:128`, `:137`,
`:448`, `:607`). A third in `OfficeView` would guarantee every future fix is
made three times.

`DOCS/ARCHITECTURE.md` row P-B already specifies `SectionReader`
(`src/ui/SectionReader.tsx`) as the shared bilingual renderer owning exactly
this, "(`SelectionAction` definition moves here)". **The file does not exist.**
This completes specified-but-unbuilt architecture — and satisfies P1.

### Reuse, do not rewrite

`alignPhrase` / `alignSelection` / `wordEcho` / `wordAtPoint`
(`src/core/text/align.ts` — `wordAtPoint(x, y)` at `:347` is exactly what
right-click-with-no-selection needs); `placeFloatingCallout` /
`reconcileCallout` (`src/core/ui/calloutPlacement.ts`); `TextLines` /
`BilingualText` / `SelectionEcho`; `annotationsFor` / `addAnnotation` /
`removeAnnotation` (`src/core/annotations/store.ts`).

### Checklist

`[ ]` not started · `[/]` in progress · `[X]` implemented · ✅ verified by running code

- `[ ]` **SR.1 Extract `SectionReader`** — `src/ui/SectionReader.tsx` (new),
  `src/ui/ReaderView.tsx` — entities: `SectionReader`, `SelectionAction`
  (definition moves here; `ReaderView` re-exports), `ReaderEntry`. Owns
  bilingual rendering, hover echo, phrase echo, callout placement, selection
  menu. `ReaderView` migrates with **no behaviour change** — Missal is the
  regression baseline.
- `[ ]` **SR.2 Migrate `BibleView`** — `src/ui/BibleView.tsx` — deletes its
  duplicate menu/echo/callout copy. `BibleWordCallout` and the BX.1R
  `reconcileCallout` termination fix must survive intact.
- `[ ]` **SR.3 Migrate `OfficeView`** — `src/ui/OfficeView.tsx` — removes
  private `OfficeText` and duplicate `bangLineClass`. **Closes #2's Breviary
  half and #10.**
- `[ ]` **SR.4 Context-menu dismissal** — `src/ui/SectionReader.tsx`,
  `src/styles.css` — entity `SectionReader.dismissMenu`. ESC and outside-click
  both close it. Today it cannot be cancelled, forcing a disruptive choice.
- `[ ]` **SR.5 Right-click targets hovered word** — reuses `wordAtPoint(x, y)`.
  No selection → the word under the cursor; with a selection → unchanged.
- `[ ]` **SR.6 Copy action** — selection when drag-selected, else the hovered
  word; `navigator.clipboard` with a non-secure-context fallback.
- `[ ]` **SR.7 Highlight action (#11)** — `src/ui/SectionReader.tsx`,
  `src/core/annotations/store.ts`, `src/styles.css` — dual-pane persisted
  highlight through the existing `mark.ann` pipeline, plus a categorised index
  of highlights and annotations.

### Known fragility to address in SR.1

The echo pairs panes by **line index**, not alignment — Latin line *N* lights
English line *N*. `alignPhrase` already computes `countsMatch` because line
counts often differ. Unmatched rubric lines change the count between panes,
which is visibly why the Gloria highlights on the Latin side and not the
English. Fixing rubric parity (#13) removes much of this; SR.1 should also fail
soft rather than highlight the wrong line when counts disagree.

